# The one discard the type system cannot see through, refused
# (design.md §4.4, §4.6; panel 118, 2026-09-08).
#
# `_ = e` throws a value away on purpose, and panel 003 put it in the
# language for exactly that. It is right for a value and wrong for a
# FAILURE: the error goes with the value, nothing in the program says
# so, and the run exits 0. Measured before this module existed:
# `_ = risky(0 - 1)` compiled and the program printed its next line.
#
# TWO POSITIONS, AND THE THIRD IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. A `_ = e` and
# a `_` parameter written `T?` are refused. A discard of a TYPE
# PARAMETER is not, and that is a ruling rather than an omission: the
# sitting first adopted it, then measured that it closes nothing —
# `function drop<A>(_: A)` swallows a failure with no discard statement
# anywhere, and refusing that too leaves `_ = [x]`, which is legal,
# means the same thing, and works on a KNOWN fallible as well. A
# refusal whose workaround defeats the rule elsewhere teaches the
# escape. So the spec states the boundary instead (§4.4): a `_` reads
# the outermost type, and `_ = args_checked()` drops a whole `[str?]`
# with no generic in sight.
#
# What this module does NOT do is decide the repair. Every fix is a
# guess and `value_errors` says why.

use ast
use source
use token
use check/state
use check/table
use value_errors

# Whether this type is a `T?`. One home for the question: `check/decls`
# asks it of a signature's result to set `Checker.fallible`, and both
# refusals below ask it of a value. A type parameter is FALSE here, see
# the module doc.
#
# A POISONED TYPE IS NOT HANDLED HERE, and the first version of this
# comment claimed it fell out by itself "because a poisoned type is not
# `.fallible`". That was false and it was caught the same hour, by
# `tests/golden/check/type-parameter-needs-context`: a `T?` whose
# payload is poisoned IS `.fallible`, so the refusal fired on a line
# that already carried `cannot_infer` and printed the type as `??`. One
# mistake, one report — so both refusals below ask `table.poisoned`
# first, which recurses into the payload.
function is_fallible(c: state.Checker, ty: i64) -> bool
    return match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .fallible                                                 => true
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit => false
        .array | .fixed | .map | .function_ty | .named            => false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic | .error                   => false

# Whether the failure's payload is a value somebody could write, which
# decides whether `.default(v)` is offered: a `()?` has none, and
# `.default(())` is `expected_expression` on §4.19's own ground.
function carries_a_payload(c: state.Checker, ty: i64) -> bool
    return match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .fallible o                                               => o.payload != table.unit_ty(c.out.types)
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit => false
        .array | .fixed | .map | .function_ty | .named            => false
        .case_ty | .failure | .generic | .error                   => false

# `_ = e`. THE TYPE IS ASKED FIRST AND THE NAME TEXT SECOND, and the
# order is the point: `source.span_text` slices the source, which
# allocates, so asking the name of every `x = …` in every checked file
# would cost one string per line for an answer the type has already
# settled. A `T?` bound to a real name is somebody keeping the failure,
# which is the correct program.
function refuse(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], name: token.Span, value: i64, ty: i64, span: token.Span)
    if table.poisoned(c.out.types, ty) || !is_fallible(c, ty)
        return

    if source.span_text(s, name) != "_"
        return
    state.push_diagnostic(
        @c,
        value_errors.discarded_failure(got: state.show(c, s, decls, ty), propagates: c.fallible, has_payload: carries_a_payload(c, ty), span: span, value: a.exprs[value].span)
    )

# A `_` parameter whose declared type is a `T?`. The same hole at the
# position where nothing can be done about it on the line, since `_`
# binds nothing and the body cannot reach the value at all.
function refuse_parameter(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], name: token.Span, ty: i64)
    if table.poisoned(c.out.types, ty) || !is_fallible(c, ty)
        return

    if source.span_text(s, name) != "_"
        return
    state.push_diagnostic(@c, value_errors.discarded_failure_parameter(got: state.show(c, s, decls, ty), span: name))
